# The deployed card, on a file whose images live in a Form XObject

Ticket 1's user-visible half was a sentence the bytes contradicted: over
`resources-xobject.pdf` — one form carrying three faces and two rasters, which MuPDF
counts on sheet 1 — the `view-metadata` card printed *"None · no raster images are
embedded"*. This is that card, on the deployed site, after `bc0bcdd`.

## Which build the site is serving

    $ curl -s https://www.browsersnip.com/ | grep -o 'assets/index-[A-Za-z0-9_-]*\.js'
    assets/index-DYSTTN5G.js

`assets/index-DYSTTN5G.js` is the chunk this tree builds (`npx vite build` at `bcf48a4`
prints the same name for the 2 347.56 kB entry), and it is a different name from the one
the site served before this pass deployed (`index-DvXje1gi.js`). Grepped apart, the two
bundles differ on the descent itself:

    $ curl -s .../assets/index-DvXje1gi.js | grep -c 'XObject/'   # before  -> 0
    $ curl -s .../assets/index-DYSTTN5G.js | grep -c 'XObject/'   # now     -> 1

## The input

`/tmp/live-form-images.pdf` = `tests/fixtures/foreign/resources-xobject.pdf` with an
`/Info` dictionary added by libqpdf 12.3.2 (`pdf.docinfo['/Title']`, saved). The added
dictionary is not decoration: `metadataReport` reaches `/Info` through `infoDict`, which
**throws** on a document that has none — the open seam
`docs/research/reader-sweep-reconciliation.md` records — so without it the card cannot
render at all, fixed walk or not. The shapes the probe depends on are unchanged by the
save, measured with MuPDF before the site saw it:

    pages 3  ·  fonts/images/xobjects per page [(3, 2, 1), (1, 0, 0), (1, 0, 0)]
    /Info present: True

Sheet 1 names one `/XObject` and nothing else; the three faces and both rasters sit in
`/Fm0`'s own `/Resources`.

## The run

    $ BASE=https://www.browsersnip.com TOOL=view-metadata \
        PDF=/tmp/live-form-images.pdf LABEL='Read metadata' \
        OUT=/tmp/live-metadata.txt PORT=9788 \
        node docs/research/rail-tool-bytes.mjs
    clicked "Read metadata"

and the screen, verbatim (`document.body.innerText`, the rig's own refusal path):

    PDF Tools / PDF Security / View Metadata
    Your file is ready — View Metadata finished in this tab; nothing was uploaded.
    Finished — nothing to download, the answer is in the report
    Read 6 section(s) of document metadata.

    File            PDF VERSION 1.7 · PAGES 3 · FILE SIZE 294.3 KiB
                    INDIRECT OBJECTS 34 · STREAMS 15 · ENCRYPTED no · BOOKMARKS 0
    Document info   TITLE Form-carried images, live probe
                    PRODUCER libqpdf 12.3.2 (Info added for the live probe)
    Pages           PAGE 1 595 × 842 pt · A4 · 29 B of content
                    PAGE 2 … 241 B · PAGE 3 … 245 B
    Fonts           FIXTURE SANS REGULAR  Type0 · embedded · full font · Identity-H
                    HELVETICA             Type1 · not embedded — substituted on screen · WinAnsiEncoding
                    NOTOSANS-REGULAR      Type0 · embedded · full font · Identity-H
    Images          40 × 24 PX  DCTDecode · 8-bit · ICCBased · 619 B · on 1 page
                    40 × 24 PX  FlateDecode · 8-bit · ICCBased · 47 B · on 1 page
    Protection      ENCRYPTED no

**The card names both rasters and all three faces, and the sentence "no raster images
are embedded" is gone** — with the sizes, filters, bit depth, colour space and stored
byte counts MuPDF and the libqpdf walk record for the same two objects in
`tests/fixtures/foreign/census.json` (40×24, 8-bit, ICCBased, 619 B DCT + 47 B Flate,
sheet 1). "on 1 page" is right too: the form is drawn once, on sheet 1, which is the
direction this session had to learn to check — a fixed inventory can also *overstate*.

## The rig, named as the suspect

`docs/research/rail-tool-bytes.mjs` ends by waiting for `URL.createObjectURL` to have
been called, and `view-metadata` on this build produced **no blob**: the rail says so on
screen ("nothing to download, the answer is in the report") and prints the sections
inline. So the probe threw `the tool produced no output blob` *after* collecting the
screen text, and `/tmp/live-metadata.txt` was never written. That is the rig's
assumption about every tool having a download, not a defect in the card — the same
script's refusal path is what captured the report quoted above. Nothing on the page
raised a console error, and the run reports `console errors []`.
